Z||000039||sloth85GT||10-07-2001||02:38 PM||||    <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Boomtastic:<BR><B>My problems are starting with a white car (or silver) and trying to get it any other color. The chosen color just seems to highlight the base color, not cover it.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ya, I was trying the same thing with my silver GT and it just won't work, is there any other way?<P>Here's the pic:<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.mts.net/~kuzma/carpics/newfrontleft-small.jpg">    <P><BR>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.mts.net/~kuzma/fieronl.gif">        <BR>Dan<BR>1985 Silver GT<p>[This message has been edited by sloth85GT (edited 10-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000040||DaRkLoRD||10-07-2001||03:47 PM||||For silver cars, you need to set the colored layer to "overlay", and also have another layer set to "darken", and then crank up the contrast on the background layer to maintain the reflections and highlights.. just play around with the layers and you'll see what I mean<P>however.. silver, gold, black & white cars never work well for colorizing, the technique I use works best on cars that already have some color. Another option is to select the body panels and then adjust the hue/saturation to get the desired color..<P>------------------<BR>     <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>     <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 10-07-2001).]||||reg||3
